// `Object(<symbol>)` wrapper object. A symbol cannot be constructed with
// `new`, but ToObject of one is a Symbol object: an *object*, so `typeof`
// answers "object" and it is never strictly equal to the primitive it holds,
// while `==` unwraps it. The checker lowers `Object(sym)` to
// `new SymbolObject(sym)`. Named like the other primitive wrappers
// (`NumberObject`, ...), for the same reason: `Symbol` is the primitive's own
// name.
//
// It declares no methods: instances share `Symbol.prototype`, whose methods
// accept the wrapper through ThisSymbolValue. Declaring `toString` here would
// have made `String(Object(sym))` answer "Symbol(x)" where the specification
// requires a TypeError -- ToPrimitive yields the symbol, and ToString of a
// symbol throws.

class SymbolObject {
    value: any;
}
